"Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110567 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110567 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1045 "Mean and Variance of the Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110839 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c970a902-f20b-5036-ab65-b55e2fe752d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186110263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111423 #edb/ref 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(μ, 3^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ) = 0.3256$, find $μ$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6217$ | $0.6255$ | $0.6293$ |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6591$ | $0.6628$ | $0.6664$ |\n| $0.5$ | $0.6950$ | $0.6985$ | $0.7019$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ$ is symmetric about the mean $μ$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02f6c3de-17f2-58af-afa6-5936538a62e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111423 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111453 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111453 #edb/ref 1061 118366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6fa2f7f1-e420-537d-bc2b-81367567a256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111453 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111454 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111454 #edb/ref 1061 118367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111454 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6ecea93-68d9-5f9e-8593-fdb0566ffc45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111454 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111455 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111455 #edb/ref 1061 118370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111455 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bf2d118-febb-52bb-99a0-706e53dac37b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111455 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111456 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111456 #edb/ref 1061 118373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111456 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b06d90dc-2c1d-585d-a385-9e6d4338a8ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111456 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111457 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111457 #edb/ref 1061 118376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111457 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e024483a-aee7-570d-b430-7d0d9873573d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111457 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111458 #edb/ref 1061 118377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111458 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "734444df-a80e-5540-b091-e90d3a989d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111458 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111916 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to calculate probabilities for normally distributed random variables $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ when $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are both *known.* \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean $\\mu$ or the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a normally distributed random variable when given a specific probability.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a normally distributed random variable $X$ with *unknown* mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$ and *known* variance ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}}^2 = {\\color{red}2}^2.$ We write this as follows:\n\n$$\nX \\sim N({\\color{blue}\\mu}, {\\color{red}2}^2)\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 2.1) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nWe can use this additional information about $X$ to calculate the mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}.$ \n\nFirst, we transform to a standard normal random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nApplying the $z$-scoring transformation to our probability statement, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.1) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}} = {\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nP\\left(Z \\leq \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{\\color{red}{2}}\\right) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $P(Z\\leq z) = \\Phi(z),$ where $\\Phi$ is the cumulative distribution function of $Z,$ we can write the above statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\\right)   = 0.7088 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.55) = 0.7088 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088) = 0.55.\n$$\n\nEquating our two expressions for $\\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088),$ we can now solve for $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.1-\\mu }{2} & =0.55 \\\\ 2.1-\\mu  & =1.1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111916 #edb/ref 1065 8989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111916 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a61536c-c1a8-53d1-a0b6-46caba6fec29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111916 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111917 #edb/ref 1064 "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma })=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma })=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <        X <       2) = P(-1 - 3 <   X <   -1 + 3) \n\\]\n\nSo, the axis of symmetry for our distribution is [math]x=-1,[/math] as shown below. \n\nUsing its symmetry, we get\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <   X <   2) = 2 P(-1 <  X <   2),\n\\]\n\nand find the cumulative probability as follows:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(-4 \\lt X \\lt  2)\t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\t2 P(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\tP(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - P(X \\lt -1) \t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - 0.5 \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\leq 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \n\\end{align*}\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by [math]z[/math]-scoring:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(X \\leq 2)\t\t\t\t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP \\left( Z \\leq \\dfrac{2 - (-1)}{\\sigma} \\right)\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\Phi \\left( \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \\right)\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \t\t\t\t\t&= \\Phi^{-1} (0.9357 ) \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\n\n-\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9207 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9332 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9345 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & \\approx 1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111917 #edb/ref 1065 8990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111917 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a748c912-a01f-5524-83df-8f7ffeb40112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111917 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Symmetry Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315690 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315690 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315690 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66a8e441-b6b2-591b-944b-3cfddb0ac398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317042 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317042 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317042 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317042 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2534596-9656-56a2-a31c-8adf543ca897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 1)=0.8413,$ use the table to find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a054739-7d5f-59e4-b190-1f466818f897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317165 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
